    <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
    <section id="introduction">

        <h2>1. Introduction to Arduino Programming</h2>

        <p>
            Arduino is one of the most popular open-source electronics platforms in
            the world. It combines easy-to-use hardware with an accessible programming
            environment, making it useful for beginners, hobbyists, students,
            engineers, makers, and professionals.
        </p>

        <p>
            An Arduino board contains a microcontroller that can be programmed to read
            inputs and control outputs. This means you can write software that reacts
            to sensors and then performs physical actions.
        </p>

        <p>
            For example, an Arduino can read a temperature sensor and automatically
            turn on a fan when the temperature becomes too high. It can detect an
            object with an ultrasonic sensor, control a servo motor, display information
            on an LCD, or communicate with another computer.
        </p>

        <h3>Why Learn Arduino Programming?</h3>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Beginner-Friendly:</strong>
                No advanced programming experience is required to begin.
            </li>

            <li>
                <strong>Hands-On Hardware:</strong>
