    <section id="getting-started">

        <h2>2. Getting Started with Arduino</h2>

        <h3>Step 1: Choose Your Arduino Board</h3>

        <p>
            Before learning how to program Arduino, you need an Arduino board.
            There are many Arduino boards available, each designed for different
            applications.
        </p>

        <p>The most common boards include:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Arduino Uno</strong> – A popular board for beginners.</li>
            <li><strong>Arduino Nano</strong> – Compact and convenient for breadboards.</li>
            <li><strong>Arduino Mega</strong> – Provides more pins and memory for larger projects.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="image-placeholder">
            <img src="images/arduino-uno-nano-mega.jpg"
                 alt="Arduino Uno Nano and Mega boards comparison">
            <p class="caption">
                Side-by-side comparison of Arduino Uno, Nano, and Mega boards.
            </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Step 2: Install the Arduino IDE</h3>

        <p>
            The Arduino IDE, or Integrated Development Environment, is the software
            where you write, compile, and upload Arduino code.
        </p>

        <p>
            Download the Arduino IDE from the official Arduino website, install it
            on your computer, and then open the application.
        </p>

        <div class="image-placeholder">
            <img src="images/arduino-ide.jpg"
                 alt="Arduino IDE interface showing Verify Upload and Serial Monitor">
            <p class="caption">
                Arduino IDE interface with Verify, Upload, and Serial Monitor tools.
            </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Step 3: Connect Your Arduino</h3>

        <p>
            Connect your Arduino board to your computer using a compatible USB cable.
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>Connect the USB cable to the Arduino.</li>
            <li>Connect the other end to your computer.</li>
            <li>Open the Arduino IDE.</li>
            <li>Select the appropriate board.</li>
            <li>Select the correct communication port.</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="image-placeholder">
            <img src="images/arduino-usb-computer.jpg"
                 alt="Arduino Uno connected to computer using USB cable">
            <p class="caption">
                Arduino Uno connected to a computer through USB.
            </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Step 4: Write Your First Program</h3>

        <p>
            Every Arduino program, commonly called a sketch, has two main functions:
            <code>setup()</code> and <code>loop()</code>.
        </p>

        <pre><code class="language-cpp">void setup() {
  pinMode(13, OUTPUT); // Set pin 13 as output
}

void loop() {
  digitalWrite(13, HIGH); // Turn LED on
  delay(1000);            // Wait 1 second
  digitalWrite(13, LOW);  // Turn LED off
  delay(1000);            // Wait 1 second
}</code></pre>

        <p>
            This simple program makes the LED on pin 13 blink approximately every second.
        </p>

