    <section id="arduino-basics">

        <h2>3. Basics of Arduino Programming</h2>

        <h3>Understanding the Structure of an Arduino Sketch</h3>

        <p>
            Every Arduino program is known as a sketch. A typical Arduino sketch
            contains two essential functions:
        </p>

        <h3><code>setup()</code></h3>

        <p>
            The <code>setup()</code> function runs once when the Arduino starts,
            powers up, or resets.
        </p>

        <p>
            It is commonly used for:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>Configuring input and output pins.</li>
            <li>Starting Serial communication.</li>
            <li>Initializing sensors.</li>
            <li>Initializing displays.</li>
            <li>Preparing libraries and hardware.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3><code>loop()</code></h3>

        <p>
            After <code>setup()</code> finishes, Arduino repeatedly executes the
            <code>loop()</code> function.
        </p>

        <p>
            This is where you normally place the main logic of your program.
        </p>

        <pre><code class="language-cpp">void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  pinMode(8, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  digitalWrite(8, HIGH);
  delay(500);

  digitalWrite(8, LOW);
  delay(500);
}</code></pre>

        <div class="image-placeholder">
            <img src="images/arduino-setup-loop-flowchart.jpg"
                 alt="Arduino setup and loop program execution flowchart">
            <p class="caption">
                Flowchart showing Arduino setup followed by the continuously repeating loop.
            </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Digital vs. Analog Pins</h3>

        <p>
            Arduino boards contain digital and analog pins. Understanding the
            difference is essential when learning Arduino programming.
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Digital Pins:</strong>
                Used to read or write digital states such as HIGH and LOW.
            </li>

            <li>
                <strong>Analog Pins:</strong>
                Used for measuring varying analog input voltages.
            </li>

            <li>
                <strong>PWM Pins:</strong>
                Supported digital pins that can provide PWM output on compatible boards.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="image-placeholder">
            <img src="images/arduino-uno-pinout.jpg"
                 alt="Arduino Uno pin layout showing digital analog and PWM pins">
            <p class="caption">
                Arduino Uno pin layout showing digital, analog, and PWM pins.
            </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Essential Arduino Functions</h3>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Function</th>
                    <th>Purpose</th>
                    <th>Example</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>

            <tbody>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>pinMode(pin, mode)</code></td>
                    <td>Sets a pin as INPUT or OUTPUT.</td>
                    <td><code>pinMode(7, OUTPUT);</code></td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>digitalWrite(pin, value)</code></td>
                    <td>Sends HIGH or LOW to a digital output.</td>
                    <td><code>digitalWrite(7, HIGH);</code></td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>digitalRead(pin)</code></td>
                    <td>Reads HIGH or LOW from a digital input.</td>
                    <td><code>int button = digitalRead(2);</code></td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>analogRead(pin)</code></td>
                    <td>Reads an analog input value.</td>
                    <td><code>int sensor = analogRead(A0);</code></td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>analogWrite(pin, value)</code></td>
                    <td>Provides PWM output on supported pins.</td>
                    <td><code>analogWrite(9, 128);</code></td>
                </tr>

            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Example: Reading a Button Input</h3>

        <pre><code class="language-cpp">int buttonPin = 2;
int ledPin = 13;

void setup() {
  pinMode(buttonPin, INPUT);
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  int buttonState = digitalRead(buttonPin);

  if (buttonState == HIGH) {
    digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);
  } else {
    digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);
  }
}</code></pre>

        <div class="image-placeholder">
            <img src="images/arduino-button-circuit.jpg"
                 alt="Arduino push button connected to pin 2 controlling LED on pin 13">
            <p class="caption">
                Push button connected to Arduino pin 2 controlling an LED.
            </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Example: Fading an LED with PWM</h3>

        <pre><code class="language-cpp">int ledPin = 9;

void setup() {
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {

  for (int brightness = 0; brightness &lt;= 255; brightness++) {
    analogWrite(ledPin, brightness);
    delay(10);
  }

  for (int brightness = 255; brightness &gt;= 0; brightness--) {
    analogWrite(ledPin, brightness);
    delay(10);
  }
}</code></pre>

